    {
      "id": "verbosity_explain_water",
      "category": "verbosity_fingerprint",
      "check_type": "length_range",
      "passed": false,
      "errored": false,
      "error": null,
      "latency_ms": 2940.0,
      "prompt_tokens": 11,
      "completion_tokens": 723,
      "output": "At its most basic level, **water** is a chemical compound made up of two elements: **hydrogen and oxygen**. Its chemical formula is **$\\text{H}_2\\text{O}$**, meaning every single molecule of water consists of two hydrogen atoms bonded to one oxygen atom.\n\nHowever, to humans, Earth, and all known life, water is much more than just a chemical formula. Here is a breakdown of what water is, why it matters, and its unique properties.\n\n---\n\n### 1. The Physical States of Water\nWater is one of the few substances on Earth that naturally exists in three distinct forms (states of matter) depending on the temperature:\n* **Liquid:** The standard form of water that fills our oceans, rivers, and rain.\n* **Solid (Ice):** Formed when water freezes at or below 0°C (32°F), found in glaciers, snow, and ice cubes.\n* **Gas (Vapor/Steam):** Formed when water evaporates or boils at 100°C (212°F), present in the air as humidity and in clouds.\n\n### 2. Unique and Miraculous Properties\nWater has several bizarre and unique scientific properties that make life possible:\n* **The \"Universal Solvent\":** Water can dissolve more substances than any other liquid. Because of this, it acts as a transport system, carrying chemicals, minerals, and nutrients wherever it goes—whether through the bloodstream of an animal or the roots of a plant.\n* **Ice Floats:** Almost all substances shrink and become denser when they freeze. Water, however, *expands* and becomes less dense as ice. This means ice floats on top of lakes and oceans, insulating the water below and allowing aquatic life to survive the winter.\n* **High Heat Capacity:** Water can absorb a lot of heat before its temperature changes significantly. This helps regulate the climate of the entire planet and keeps living organisms from overheating.\n* **Cohesion and Adhesion:** Water molecules like to stick to each other (cohesion—which creates surface tension, allowing some bugs to walk on water) and to other things (adhesion—which helps water move up from the roots of a tall tree to its highest leaves).\n\n### 3. Water on Earth\n* **Coverage:** About **71% of the Earth's surface** is covered by water, mostly in oceans and seas.\n* **Scarcity:** Despite the Earth looking blue and wet, **97.5% of Earth's water is salt water** (found in oceans and un-drinkable). Only **2.5% is fresh water**, and most of that is locked away in glaciers, ice caps, or buried deep underground. Less than 1% of Earth's water is easily accessible for human use.\n\n### 4. Why Water Matters for Life\nBiologically speaking, **life equals water.** \n* The human body is made up of roughly **60% water**.\n* Your brain is about 73% water, your lungs are about 83%, and even your bones are about 31% water.\n* Water regulates our internal body temperature, lubricates joints, flushes out waste, and provides the moist environment chemical reactions need to happen inside our cells. \n\nIn short, water is the driving force of nature, weather, climate, and biology. Without it, life as we know it simply could not exist."
